// Real place-bound legends and stories, for the map prototype.
//
// Place-bound rule: a story is included only if it is meaningfully attached to a real, identifiable place
// (a town, castle, building, monument, mountain, cave, forest, lake, river, island, bridge, or site),
// ideally one that is essential to the story rather than an incidental setting. Famous myths with no
// specific geographic anchor (e.g. "Greek mythology" or "Arthurian legends" in general) don't qualify;
// their place-specific traditions (Knossos, Tintagel) do. `place` names the anchor.
//
// Sources, preferred in this order: folklore archives and academic collections; national or regional
// heritage institutions; museums, libraries, historical societies; municipal heritage; published folklore
// collections; tourism and cultural websites; Wikipedia only for discovery. Stories whose `source.type`
// is still "encyclopedia" are waiting for a better source. `source.placeNamed` is the place as the
// source names it; `source.confidence` is how sure the geographic attribution is.
//
// Coordinates come from the Wikipedia article for the site (used only for location). Where only a town or
// region is documented, locationType is "area" or "uncertain". Descriptions are short retellings in our
// own words.
import type { MapStory } from '../mapStories'

